CHAIRMAN'S MESSAGE
Vietnam's economy showed remarkable resiliency in 2024. Thanks to the recovery of the
manufacturing and export processing sectors and fiscal stimulus measures, the country's gross
domestic product (GDP) grew by 7.09%ot over the course of the year, surpassing the target
growth rate of 6 to 6.5%o and ranking among the top nations in the region and the world.The
banking industry saw significant success in 2024 as wetl, with credit gro*h increasing by
around 15.08%2, lending interest rates falling by 0.44%, and capital mobilization rising by
9.06Yo3 compared to the end of 2023. Also, exchange rates remained relatively stable.
Additionally, the inflation rate (CPl) was controlled at an average of 3.63%a for the year, much
lower than the target of 4Yo.
In the context ofstable macroeconomic conditions but with some local areas severely affected
by natural disasters, businesses encountering difficulties due to competitive pressures, and the
banking sector participating in supporting barriers, ACB continues to grow its total assets and
maintain good profitability, specifi cally:
- Customer deposits reached over VND 537 trillion, an increase of ll.3o/o.lncluding funds
raised from valuable papers issuance, the total mobilized capital amounted to VND 639
trillion, up 19.4%o compared to the beginning of the year. As a result, the CASA ratio
increased to 23.3o/o, increasing by 0.4% compared to the previous year.
- Customer loans reached VND 581 trillion, increasingby 19.1% compared to the end of
2023, exceeding the industry's average growth rate for nine consecutive years. Especially,
the corporate loans increased by 25%o compared to the end of2023.
- Non-performing loan (NPL) ratio remained at l.49Yo, one of the lowest in the industry.
- Retum on equity (ROE) continued to reach a high level of 2l.7Yo.
In 2025, Vietnam's economy is forecasted to continue facing numerous challenges, including
a slow global economic recovery, intense competition in export markets, and trade wars
between major economies due to increasing trade barriers and tariffs. However, based on the
economy's intemal strengths, including domestic consumption, business investment, public
investment, trade surplus, and the Govemment's policies, the expectation that Vietnam,s GDP
growth in 2025 would rcach \yo or higher with an average inflation rate of around 4.5-5yo5 is
